{
  "image_name": "1e1f2e5bab91e4e2d4cca748e9beb04f.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how disruption of normal daily rhythms and feeding patterns can influence cancer through host neuroendocrine and immune pathways. On the left, the usual day–night cycle and feeding–fasting cycle are crossed out, indicating circadian and metabolic disruption. These disturbances act on the host, especially through the brain and autonomic nervous system.\n\nSignals from the SCN and pineal gland are shown feeding into sympathetic nerve activity and melatonin production, both of which affect tumours. Additional pathways connect sympathetic nerves to the adrenal glands, spleen, and bone marrow. These organs then influence circulating factors such as lymphocytes, tumour immunity, glucocorticoids, and inflammatory or metabolic mediators like IL‑6 and lactate. The overall message is that altered rhythms do not act on cancer directly only, but reshape the body’s hormonal, neural, and immune environment in ways that can support tumour behaviour.\n\nThe right side highlights several cancers—lung, pancreatic, liver, and colorectal—suggesting these mechanisms are relevant across multiple tumour types. Chemotherapy is also included as a factor interacting with these host pathways, implying treatment responses may be shaped by the same circadian, immune, and endocrine mechanisms.",
